<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Creticus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 54 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Metellus Creticus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Creticus was a Roman general and consul in 69 BCE. Sent against the Cretan cities, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> accused of aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, he besieged Kydonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and other towns between 69 and 67 BCE and conquered the whole island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> then became a Roman province, later joined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, with Gortyn as its capital, and he took the surname Creticus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born around 114 BCE into the Caecilii Metelli, a prominent and conservative family of the plebeian gens Caecilia; a saying attributed to Naevius held that the Metelli were fated to become consuls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His grandfather was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Macedonicus</a>, and his father Gaius Caecilius Metellus Caprarius, consul in 113 BCE. His sister Caecilia Metella married Gaius Verres, and his daughter, also Caecilia Metella, married a son of Marcus Crassus; her tomb still survives on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appian-way">Via Appia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was praetor in 74 BCE and a pontifex from 73 BCE until his death. Elected consul with Quintus Hortensius Hortalus, he was a friend and supporter of Verres before his trial in 70 BCE. Cicero, prosecuting for the Sicilians, accused him of telling the Sicilian witnesses that Verres would come to no harm because of the offices he and his brothers held, and suggested that he had won his office through Verres's bribery.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> was accused of supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> to Mithridates of Pontus and of alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and it had refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s terms. Given the command after Hortensius declined it, Metellus defeated the Cretan commander Lasthenes at Kydonia, which Panares surrendered, and laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. In 67 BCE the Cretans offered their surrender to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, who held a command against piracy under the Lex Gabinia and ordered Metellus to leave. Metellus refused, took the last stronghold, Lappa, despite Pompey's men there, and declared Crete a province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and his allies delayed his triumph until 62 BCE. In return Metellus used his influence to block the senate's ratification of Pompey's settlement of the east until 60 BCE, and he remained a prominent opponent of Pompey until his death. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s letters to Atticus, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 60 BCE with Lucius Flaccus and Lentulus to keep the Gallic states from joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>. Juvenal later used his cognomen in his eighth satire to argue that virtue, not an inherited name, makes one noble.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quintus Caecilius Metellus Creticus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Rome turned on Crete, the Cretans defeated Marcus Antonius at sea near Kydonia around 71 BCE, but <b>Quintus Caecilius Metellus</b> then reduced the cities one by one, including Knossos, between 69 and 67 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After years of fighting under <b>Metellus</b> and then Marius, Jugurtha fled to his father-in-law, Bocchus I of the Kingdom of Mauretania, who in 105 BCE handed him over to Marius's quaestor Sulla.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome's war on piracy brought Marcus Antonius' failed attack in 71 BCE and then <b>Quintus Caecilius Metellus</b>' conquest of the island in 69-67 BCE, after which Crete became a Roman province with Gortyn as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/cretan-koinon">Cretan Koinon</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Metellus</b> captured Eleutherna during the Roman conquest of Crete in 68 or 67 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/eleutherna">Eleutherna</a></li><li><blockquote>Hierapytna was among the last Cretan cities to fall to <b>Metellus</b> in 67 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/hierapytna">Hierapytna</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Quintus Caecilius Metellus</b> besieged and took Kydonia in 69 BCE on his way to conquering the island, which became part of the province of Crete and Cyrene.</blockquote><a href="/state/kydonia">Kydonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-64" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Quintus Caecilius Metellus Creticus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-64">What was here in 64 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
